natambio (1.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New per-cycle timing counters and the "timecycle" remote command. The JACK
    callback is timed end to end, the convolution stage within it (the input
    sums, zita-convolver's process() and the delay/scale/mix on the way out),
    and every <nae> engine's block in its own thread. Each keeps the last 1000
    cycles in a FIFO, and "timecycle" reports the mean, the sample deviation,
    the shortest and the longest of them in microseconds, plus the mean as a
    percentage of the period, which is reported in a small table of its own so
    the figures can be read without knowing the configuration.
  * Remote: the report is two tab-separated tables, each under a header line
    naming its columns -- seven numbers in a row are unreadable without one.
    Header lines begin with "#" and data lines with "ok", so a caller skips the
    headers as it already skips the "muted" line of a "get" and reads the rest
    with awk -F'\t'; column -t -s $'\t' lines the answer up on a terminal.
  * Remote: every "timecycle" stage row carries two counts, the cycles timed
    since the last reset and how many of them the figures cover. The first
    matters for the NAE engines: the callback signals them once per period and
    does not wait, so an engine whose count trails the callback's is one that
    is not keeping up, which no mean of the periods it did get would show.
    "timecycle reset" empties every history, so a measurement can be made to
    start when the music does rather than where the last one left off.
  * The counters are always on: two clock_gettime() per stage per period, vDSO
    reads of a few tens of nanoseconds against a period of a thousand
    microseconds or more. The history is a fixed ring of std::atomic written
    with no allocation, no lock and nothing that can block (src/cycletime.hpp);
    it is read without synchronisation, a snapshot being a cheaper price than a
    lock in the real-time path for a statistic over a thousand samples.

 -- Raul Fernandez Ortega <natambio.audio@gmail.com>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

natambio (1.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * XTC: the model delay is now derived from the sample rate instead of being
    a fixed 64 samples. It is a duration -- 1.33 ms -- and what has to be
    compensated for elsewhere is the time, so 1.1.0's fixed tap count made
    that compensation move with the rate: 64 taps are 1.45 ms at 44.1 kHz and
    0.67 ms at 96 kHz. xtc_model_delay() (lib/xtc.c) scales it: 59 samples at
    44.1 kHz, 64 at 48, 128 at 96. A system with a subwoofer fed on a path
    that bypasses XTC compensates one figure in milliseconds and it holds at
    every rate; a 96 kHz configuration written against 1.1.0 was compensating
    64 samples where the design added 64 but should have added 128.
  * XTC: scaling costs almost nothing in accuracy and gains at high rates.
    Measured on the cross filter (ITD 180 us, 4096 taps) the discarded
    pre-ring goes from -54.7 dB with a fixed 64 to -54.1 dB with the scaled 59
    at 44.1 kHz, and from -83.0 to -87.7 dB with the scaled 128 at 96 kHz.
  * XTC: the configuration report prints the model delay with its duration,
    and the <length> lower bound is reported with the rate it was computed at,
    since the bound moves with the rate.
  * Tools: natambio-xtc-filters derives its model delay from the sample rate
    too when neither -M nor a model_delay key is given, so a filter generated
    offline carries the latency natambio would have added to the same design.
    An explicit -M or key still wins, and a negative one is still refused.

 -- Raul Fernandez Ortega <natambio.audio@gmail.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0200

natambio (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NAE: new <pan_scale>, a width control on the engine's input pair -- a
    power-complementary mid/side matrix applied before anything looks at the
    signal, ahead of the decomposition and of the correlation the beta mode
    weighting rides on. +1 collapses the pair to mono, 0 leaves it untouched,
    -1 puts the two channels in opposite polarity. Read in both modes. An
    exploratory control: it promises no improvement in quality, and leaving it
    at 0 is a perfectly good answer.
  * NAE: internal naming reworked around the two components (c1, c2), and the
    beta mode output called ambient rather than surround.
  * XTC: new <frac_delay> in <xtc> and <xtc_asym>, which runs the crosstalk
    recursion at the exact ITD in the frequency domain instead of rounding it
    to whole samples. The rounding capped the design's own suppression in the
    top octaves -- at 48 kHz an itd_us of 180 is 8.64 samples and became 9,
    around -27 dB above 8 kHz, where the exact ITD reaches the ladder's floor
    some 55 dB lower. It matters more in <xtc_asym>, which rounds each side
    independently, so the round-trip period inherits both errors. Optional
    and default false, so existing configurations keep producing the same
    coefficients.
  * XTC: new <model_delay> (default 64 samples), the bulk delay the fractional
    path adds to all its filters so that a two-sided fractional shift is not
    clipped at n = 0. Ignored unless <frac_delay>; given without it, it now
    warns instead of being read and silently dropped.
  * New <remote> block: a TCP command socket, one command per line, that moves
    gains and NAE parameters while natambio runs. The tag's content is the
    port; there is no default, so a configuration that says nothing about
    remote control gets no socket. natambio refuses to start if the port
    cannot be bound. Bound to 127.0.0.1, and the commands carry no credential
    of any kind, so driving it from another machine means an SSH forward.
  * Remote: "up" and "down" move named ports' gains relative to what they are
    now, "upin"/"downin"/"upout"/"downout" move every input or every output
    port bodily, and "get" reports the gains as they stand. Results are
    clamped to [-120, +20] dB and slewed at the start/stop fade rate rather
    than applied as a step. A command that fails changes and reports nothing.
  * Remote: "mute" takes every output to -120 dB and leaves the ports' own
    gains alone, so up/down go on working through it; "unmute" brings them
    back and "toggle" is the two of them for a caller with a single button.
    The input ports are deliberately left running, so the convolution tails
    and the NAE window are not drained into silence.
  * Remote: "naegain" sets an NAE engine's <front_gain>, <ambience_gain> and
    <rear_gain> by the engine's <name>, and "naepan" its <pan_scale>. Both
    are absolute rather than relative, being a balance and a width rather
    than a volume; naepan refuses a value outside [-1, +1] instead of
    quietly clamping it.
  * Remote: "naeget" reports one engine's configuration as its XML block, and
    "getxmlconfig" the whole live configuration, valid to start another
    natambio with. Every command that changes something writes the new value
    back into the configuration as well as into the running audio, so there
    is one place holding what the system is set to.
  * The <port>s of <jack_input> and <jack_output> take a <gain> in dB
    (default 0 dB, following the common gain convention), which is what the
    remote's gain commands move.
  * XTC: the design tags <frac_delay> and <model_delay> were retired. The
    recursion always runs at the exact, unrounded ITD, and the bulk delay that
    path needs is fixed at 64 samples, so there is nothing left to choose: the
    fractional path was the one worth using and the model delay had one
    sensible value. A configuration still carrying either tag is warned that
    it is ignored, and one carrying it inside an <xtc_asym> side block is
    still a parse error. <length> must now exceed the 64-sample model delay,
    which the parser checks and names. get_xtc() and process()'s frac_delay
    argument stay in lib/xtc.c, and the offline tools keep their own switch.
  * XTC: get_xtc() wrote cross_out[n] without checking n against the filter
    length. The ladder starts at (NSTEPS-1)*delay, so any configuration where
    31*ITD reached the filter length corrupted the heap on the first design
    call. The rest of the arithmetic is bounded while there: index math in
    long long with the delay capped so the ladder start and the 2*delay step
    cannot overflow, filter_len and sample_rate bounded so next_pow2() cannot
    shift into the sign bit, fft_convolve_truncate()'s return value checked,
    and process() validating pointers, ranges and finiteness. ild_db must now
    be a positive dB step -- that is what keeps the ladder convergent, and a
    zero or negative value turned the per-step attenuations into gains and ran
    away to +inf. A filter_len too short for the requested ITD is warned
    about, since dropped taps mean cancellation that quietly does not cancel.
  * DSP: the filter design math moved from the single- to the double-precision
    FFTW API. minimum_phase() takes log|X| of a linear-phase FIR that has a
    forced zero at Nyquist, where |X| lands on the transform's own noise floor
    -- around 1e-5 in single precision, seven orders of magnitude above
    LOG_EPS, so log|X| returned the log of the rounding error and get_xtc()
    fed that through sixteen chained convolutions. Also hypot() there, whose
    squares underflowed at exactly those bins. Filter coefficients stay single
    precision: naconf.cpp narrows to float for zita-convolver, which is the
    right boundary. Only the design math is double, and it runs offline at
    configuration time, never on the RT path. Measured against the resulting
    filters, the previous output was off by about 2e-2 relative L2 on the
    cross filter.
  * DSP: minimum_phase() oversamples the cepstral transform. The complex
    cepstrum of a FIR has infinite support and decays only as 1/k, so running
    it on a transform the length of the filter wrapped its tail back onto
    itself and exp() turned that aliasing into a magnitude error -- 0.2 dB max
    deviation from the linear-phase response it has to match, which those same
    sixteen convolutions amplified into roughly 5% on the delivered filters.
    At eight times the length, zero-padded and truncated back, the deviation
    is 0.0004 dB.

 -- Raul Fernandez Ortega <natambio.audio@gmail.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

natambio (1.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial Debian packaging.
  * Split into natambio (real-time JACK client), natambio-tools (offline C
    FIR/DRC generators), natambio-ladspa (LADSPA plugin) and natambio-drc
    (offline Python toolkit).

